#use(std/code)
#use(std/table)

#section{Yet Another Mark Down language}
	Indentation-based markup language compiling to an element IR (HTML backend
	first).

	Inspiration, without any particular order:

	#list
		#item: typst, latex
		#item: ruby, haml, erb

	Non-goals: being HTML; being tied to Ruby. The host language is pluggable in
	principle, but a document is a program in #emph{one} host (reference implementation: Racket).

	#section{Core principles}
		#list
			#item: #strong{One sigil.} #code{#} is the only globally special character. Every other symbol (#code{|}, #code{*}, #code{$}, …) is plain text unless a surrounding function's reader gives it meaning.
			#item: #strong{One form per role.} Call, comment, escape — nothing else. Headings, lists, links, tables, math are all library functions, not syntax.
			#item: #strong{Documents are programs.} The sigil escapes from text mode into host mode; #code{{}} escapes back. Turing completeness comes from the host, not from a template mini-language.
			#item: #strong{Two-pass evaluation} (collect → resolve) for cross-references, TOC, numbering, glossaries.

	#section{Syntax}
		#section{Text mode}
			Plain text is content. Structure is defined by indentation (tabs). Lines
			at a given indent level belong to the enclosing block.

			#table(header: 1)
				#row
					#cell: Form
					#cell: Meaning
				#row
					#cell: #code{#name(args)}
					#cell: Call. #code{args} is host code (keyword args welcome; no commas — #code{,} is Racket unquote. #code{#use} alone tolerates them).
				#row
					#cell: #code|{#name(args){body}}|
					#cell: Call with inline body. Body is markup by default; parsed to content and passed as the last argument. Braces must balance.
				#row
					#cell: #code{#name(args): text}
					#cell: Call with body = rest of line.
				#row
					#cell: #code{#name(args):} at end of line
					#cell: Call with body = following indented block. The #code{:} is optional (see the row below); with an inline body a #code{:} and nothing indented below is literal text, not a body.
				#row
					#cell: #code{#name(args)::}
					#cell: Call with body = rest of the #strong{enclosing block} (see Hanging blocks).
				#row
					#cell: #code|{#name(args){body}}| alone on a line
					#cell: Body = the #code|{{body}}| plus the following indented block (the trailing #code{:} is optional sugar; #code|{#section{Title}}| and #code|{#section{Title}:}| are the same form).
				#row
					#cell: #code{#name(args)} alone on a line
					#cell: Sugar for #code{#name(args):} — body = following indented block.
				#row
					#cell: #code{#(expr)}
					#cell: Host escape. Statement position → statement (e.g. #code{#(define …)}); inline → expression, result converted to content.
				#row
					#cell: #code{##}
					#cell: Literal #code{#}.
				#row
					#cell: #code{# }, #code{#;} / #code|{#;{…}}|
					#cell: Line / block comment.

			#code{(args)} and #code|{{body}}| are each optional. #code{#name} bare
			is a zero-arg call.

		#section{Host mode}
			Inside #code{#(...)} and #code{(args)} you are in the host language.
			#code|{#name{…}}| / #code|{#…{markup}}| re-enters text mode, producing a
			content value (Scribble semantics). Example:

			#code(lang: "plain")
				#(for ([x items])
					#p{#(item-name x) — #(item-price x)¥})

		#section{Variables, metadata}
			#code(lang: "plain")
				#(define price 42)
				The item costs #(price).

				#(meta 'date "2025-01-01")   ; writes document state (collect pass)
				Published: #(get-meta 'date) ; reads on resolve pass — order-independent

			Scoping is host scoping; defines inside a block are local to that block.

	#section{Blocks and indentation}
		#code(lang: "plain")
			#list(style: "1")
				#item: First
				#item
					Second, multi-line
					#list(style: "a")
						#item: nested

		#list
			#item: A block body is the following lines indented one level deeper. A call alone on its line takes it — whether the call is bare (#code{#foo}) or already carries an inline body (#code|{#section{Title}}|). A trailing #code{:} is optional sugar: #code{#foo}, #code{#foo:}, #code|{#section{Title}}|, and #code|{#section{Title}:}| each take the block the same way. (With an inline body, a #code{:} and #emph{nothing} indented below is literal text, not an empty body — so prose like #code{#code{index.yamd}:} stays intact.)
			#item: Dedent closes the block. Nesting = indentation; no #code{"-.1"}-style encodings needed (a #code{list} function may still accept them as sugar — its business).
			#item: Indentation is tabs only, single tab per level.

		#section{Hanging blocks (::)}
			#code{::} folds the #emph{rest of the current block} into the call's
			body, without indentation:

			#code(lang: "plain")
				#section{Motivation}::

				Everything until the enclosing dedent/EOF is inside the section.
				#note: including this.

			#list
				#item: Pure reader-level transform; evaluation sees an ordinary body.
				#item: Recursive: a later #code{::} at the same level nests inside the earlier one. Flat LaTeX-style sectioning, if wanted, is implemented by the #code{section} function (level-aware), not by the reader.
				#item: #code{::} at top level = rest of file (useful for #code{#template(...)::}).

	#section{Contexts}
		Host-language tweaks live in #strong{contexts} that are auto-scoped: by
		default all settings go to the current context. #code{#fork-context::}
		forks the current context for the rest of the enclosing block; block-bodied
		calls fork implicitly where it makes sense. Contexts also drive
		auto-detection, e.g. #code{section} stores the current header level in the
		context, so nesting depth determines #code{<h1>}/#code{<h2>}/… without
		explicit levels.

	#section{Body readers (the extensibility primitive)}
		A function may register a #strong{reader} for its body, consulted
		#emph{before} parsing:

		#table(header: 1)
			#row
				#cell: Reader
				#cell: Behavior
			#row
				#cell: #code{markup} (default)
				#cell: Full YAMD parsing.
			#row
				#cell: #code{raw}
				#cell: Verbatim text. Block form: dedented, terminated only by dedent — no delimiter scanning at all. Inline form: brace-balanced scan, no #code{#} recognition.
			#row
				#cell: custom
				#cell: Plugin-supplied parser (tables, math notations, …).

		Examples:

		#code(lang: "plain")
			#code(lang: python)
				def f(): # not a comment, raw
					return {"x": 1}

			Inline #tt{x = 1} and math #m{sum_(i=0)^n i^2}.

		Context-scoped symbols keep the global symbol budget at one.

		#section{Fences for pathological inline content}
			Inline raw/markup bodies with unbalanced braces use alternative
			delimiters (Scribble-style): #code|={|{ … }|}=|, extensible fence:

			#code(lang: "plain")
				#tt|{ unbalanced } here }|
				#tt|==={ even }| inside }===|

			Opener #code|{|X{}| closes only at #code|{}X|}|. Block raw bodies never
			need this.

	#section{Plugins}
		#list
			#item: A plugin is a host module exporting functions (+ optional body readers).
			#item: #code{#use(std/table, std/math: asciimath)} imports/binds; math notation (AsciiMath / LaTeX / typst-math) is chosen by the bound plugin — the core knows nothing about math.
			#item: Project-level #emph{reader macros} (e.g. backtick spans → #code{#tt{…}}) are allowed as opt-in sugar on top of existing functions; never new core forms.

	#section{Tables}
		Mainly support HTML-like cells:

		#code(lang: "plain")
			#table(header: 1)
				#row
					#cell: Name
					#cell: Role
				#row{Kiril}{infra}

		#list
			#item: #code|{#row{a}{b}}| positional multi-body form is supported.
			#item: A #emph{source-beautiful} pipe-table plugin (#code{Name | Role | Since}) exists as a separate plugin and is allowed to be pretty dumb.

	#section{Cross-references / glossary (stdlib, via two-pass)}
		#code(lang: "plain")
			#def("vm"){virtual machine}{A sandboxed execution environment.}
			…deterministic #ref("vm") semantics…
			#glossary

		#code{def} records in collect; #code{ref}/#code{glossary} resolve in the
		second pass. Same machinery serves TOC, numbering, citations, links and
		backlinks.

	#section{Rendering}
		Functions emit an #strong{element IR}, not HTML strings. Backends (HTML
		first) serialize the IR. Raw-HTML escape hatch is a function in the HTML
		backend's namespace, clearly marked non-portable.

	#section{Implementation notes (Racket plan)}
		#list
			#item: #code{#lang yamd}: indentation reader on top of at-exp-style parsing.
			#item: Scribble gives at-notation, fences, and the collect/resolve architecture nearly for free; write the indentation layer + IR + HTML backend.
			#item: Body-reader dispatch: compile-time table / syntax property on the function, consulted by the reader.
			#item: If the host ever changes, the surface syntax and reader design survive; only #code{#(...)} contents and the plugin ABI are host-specific.
			#item: Prefer Typed Racket with type assertions where it makes sense.

	#section{Consciously accepted tradeoffs}
		#list
			#item: Documents are programs: no sandboxed rendering of untrusted documents; incremental/parallel builds are harder.
			#item: One host at a time: a Ruby YAMD and a Racket YAMD would be different languages sharing a surface syntax.
			#item: #code{#} collides with hex colors, "issue ##42", shebangs in prose — mitigated by #code{##} and by code living in raw bodies. (If the host were Ruby, #code|{#{}}|/#code{#} comment collisions would argue for a different sigil; for Racket it's fine.)

	#section{Resolved questions}
		#list
			#item: Indentation: tabs only, single tab per level.
			#item: #code{args} grammar: full host expressions.
			#item: #code{::}: no "until next same-name #code{::}" flat variant for now.
			#item: Inline math: #code{#m{…}} only, no reader-macro shorthand.
			#item: Error reporting: best effort mapping of IR/runtime errors back to source lines through the indentation reader.
